---
title: Links and references
order: 30
description: How internal links, fragments and the table of contents work.
---

Links are the heart of a documentation site, so quaydoc spends extra care on
them. Every internal link is validated at build-and-check time, and headings
are directly linkable by their title.

## Fragment links

Each heading in a page gets an `id` attribute derived from its title. A
heading titled `Installation` becomes an `<h2 id="installation">`, and you
can link straight to it with a bare fragment link:

```
[[Installation]]
```

The reference resolves to the page that contains a heading with that title
(after normalising case and whitespace) plus its fragment. If two pages
contain the same heading, the first page wins in document order.

## Page references

A `[[...]]` reference whose target is a page title — the `title` front
matter value — links to that page's root URL:

```
[[Writing pages]]
```

If the same words name both a page and a heading, the heading wins.

## Relative paths

Explicit paths work too, relative to the current page:

```
[[../topics/search.qd|Search page]]
[guide reference](../reference/cli.qd)
![logo](../../assets/logo.svg)
```

Paths are resolved against the source tree at build time, and the checker
verifies that a built page exists for every path link.

## The checker

`quaydoc check` reads the rendered HTML and reports, by page:

- `broken-fragment`: a `#fragment` with no matching id anywhere on the
  target page,
- `missing-page`: an internal link whose target page was not built,
- `missing-image`: an image source that does not exist,
- `duplicate-id`: an id used more than once on one page.

Keeping heading ids and fragment targets in lock-step is what makes the
first check pass; the two sides of the pipeline are documented in the
source as needing to agree exactly.

## Table of contents

Every page with more than one heading gets an "On this page" panel on the
right, listing its sections as jump links. The same anchor ids are used by
in-page references, so jumping and linking agree.
